WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.347
Some things are long. Some things
are heavy. Some hold a lot.

2
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:12.701
Measuring tells you exactly how
long, how heavy, or how much.

3
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:16.637
Here are a pencil and a
crayon. Which one is longer?

4
00:00:16.857 --> 00:00:20.247
They both start in the same
place, on the same line.

5
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:24.879
Now look at where each one ends.
The pencil ends further along.

6
00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:28.257
So the pencil is longer,
and the crayon is shorter.

7
00:00:28.477 --> 00:00:33.492
A fat thing is not a long thing.
Only the length along it counts.

8
00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:38.218
You can measure with cubes. Lay
them along the pencil, with no gaps.

9
00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:42.339
Then count them: one, two,
three, four, five, six, seven.

10
00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:46.460
The pencil is seven cubes
long. Cubes are the unit here.

11
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.767
Every cube must be the same
size, or the count means nothing.

12
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:55.725
A ruler measures in centimetres.
The marks are all the same size.

13
00:00:55.945 --> 00:00:59.660
Line up the end of the pencil
with the 0, not with the 1.

14
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.410
Read the number where the
pencil ends. It ends at the 8.

15
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.159
So the pencil is 8 centimetres
long. You measured it.

16
00:01:07.379 --> 00:01:12.581
To draw a line 6 centimetres long,
start at 0 and stop at the 6.

17
00:01:13.081 --> 00:01:18.700
Now choose a unit that fits. A longer
pencil is about 15 centimetres.

18
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.728
A door is about 2 metres
tall. Big things need metres.

19
00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:27.778
An apple is light, so grams. A
school bag is heavy, so kilograms.

20
00:01:27.998 --> 00:01:32.270
A spoon of medicine is millilitres.
A bath of water is litres.

21
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:36.625
Mass is how heavy a thing
is. A balance can find it.

22
00:01:36.845 --> 00:01:40.560
The apple side tips down, so
the apple is the heavier side.

23
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.820
Add 100 grams, then 200
grams. Now the beam is level.

24
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.173
Level means both sides match.
The apple is 300 grams.

25
00:01:49.393 --> 00:01:54.037
Capacity is how much a jug holds.
Each mark is 100 millilitres.

26
00:01:54.257 --> 00:01:58.298
Pour in three times, and the
water reaches the 300 mark.

27
00:01:58.798 --> 00:02:03.302
A measuring scale is a number line,
and not every mark has a number.

28
00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:06.216
Here the numbered marks say 30 and 40.

29
00:02:06.436 --> 00:02:10.801
One small mark sits halfway
between them. It is worth 5 more.

30
00:02:11.021 --> 00:02:15.154
The ribbon ends on that mark,
so it is 35 centimetres long.

31
00:02:15.654 --> 00:02:19.602
Start at 0, use one unit all
the way, and read where it ends.

32
00:02:19.822 --> 00:02:23.676
Small things take small units.
Big things take big ones.

33
00:02:23.896 --> 00:02:29.515
A balance finds mass, a jug finds
capacity, and every mark counts.
